Let a process take the turn when its holder is not interested, wait only while the holder is interested, and always pass the turn on:

```pascal
flag[i] := true;
while flag[j] or flag[k] do
  if turn <> i then
    if flag[turn] then
      begin
        flag[i] := false;
        while (turn <> i) and flag[turn] do skip;
        flag[i] := true
      end
    else turn := i;          { holder idle: claim the turn }
critical section
turn := j;                   { pass on cyclically: i -> j -> k -> i }
flag[i] := false;
```
The flag protocol is unchanged, so mutual exclusion still holds by (a); the turn always names an interested process or is claimed, so the spinning deadlock disappears. (A fully bounded-waiting n-process version is the Eisenberg–McGuire algorithm.)
